1,null,0.9194449047847595,null,1.0383537289434104,null,1.1323775008553418,null,1.185262370616236,null,1.1908946000116296,null,1.1535473296191148,null,1.0860306365167014,null,1.006257264896041,null,0.9330324603479572,null,0.8819900854310934,null,0.8625126900068212,null,0.8762171011906985,null,0.9172214512776709,null,0.9740172933873859,null,1.032436733390122,null,1.0789982957754118,null,1.103874986598776,null,1.102854481769138,null,1.0779189518741945,null,1.0363980042314294,null,0.9889675780505132,null,0.9470101165589047,null,0.9199667940669642,null,0.9132812314927032,null,0.9273698001068906,null,0.9577992421815846,null,0.996569242639757,null,1.0341509483969176,null,1.0617764741558608,null],"role":"reference"}],"worst":9.992007221626409e-16,"worst_label":"largest |difference|","note":"This row checks the reference, not PyStratify: the Sommerfeld integrals written without PyStratify (emitter_references.py), given a perfect mirror (r_s = −1, r_p = 1), must give the image dipole's closed form, Drexhage's oscillations; a magnetic dipole sees the mirror with the opposite sign. The same integrals are then PyStratify's reference for real stacks (the Sommerfeld row, 1e-10).","script":"benchmarks/pages/films_dipole_consistency.py","function":"plot_perfect_mirror","rmse":1.9547493470172272e-16,"summary":"the Sommerfeld reference itself against the image dipole of a perfect mirror","aside":"Given a perfect mirror, $r_s = -1$ and $r_p = 1$, the Sommerfeld integrals must reproduce the image dipole (Drexhage; Chance, Prock & Silbey), with $x = kd = 4\\pi h/\\lambda$:\n\n$$\\frac{\\Gamma_\\perp}{\\Gamma_0} = 1 + 3\\left(\\frac{\\sin x}{x^3} - \\frac{\\cos x}{x^2}\\right)$$\n\n$$\\frac{\\Gamma_\\parallel}{\\Gamma_0} = 1 - \\frac{3}{2}\\left(\\frac{\\sin x}{x} + \\frac{\\cos x}{x^2} - \\frac{\\sin x}{x^3}\\right)$$\n\nA magnetic dipole sees the mirror with the opposite sign, $2 - \\Gamma_\\parallel/\\Gamma_0$. This checks the reference every other film-emitter row relies on."}
